<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nidaros Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1070–1300</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nidaros Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a> is the national sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, built between about 1070 and 1300 over the grave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Saint Olaf</a>. The northernmost medieval cathedral in the world, it combines Romanesque and Gothic styles and was the goal of pilgrims from across the Nordic world. It became the seat of an archbishop in 1152–1153, and it has been the setting for royal coronations and consecrations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral.jpg" alt="Nidaros Cathedral" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DXR · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nidaros_Cathedral,_Trondheim,_West_view_20150605_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
